### Why does synchronous REST fail for long-running work?

A server has a bounded thread pool. In synchronous REST, one request occupies one thread from arrival until response. For a workflow that takes seconds or minutes, that thread is held the whole time, and it is mostly idle waiting on I/O. You can exhaust all threads while the CPU sits at five percent.

```java
package com.bank.transfer;

import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PostMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestBody;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@RestController
public class SyncTransferController {

    // WRONG: holds thread for entire international transfer
    @PostMapping("/api/transfers/international")
    public TransferResult transfer(@RequestBody TransferRequest req) {
        // Step 1: validate (10ms)
        validate(req);
        // Step 2: fraud check (2s)
        fraudService.check(req.getFrom(), req.getAmount());
        // Step 3: SWIFT messaging (5-30s)
        swiftGateway.send(req);
        // Step 4: correspondent bank (10-60s)
        correspondentBank.settle(req);
        // Thread blocked for up to 90 seconds!
        return TransferResult.success();
    }
}
```

```text
Thread pool exhaustion:
  Pool size: 200 threads
  Each international transfer: 30-90 seconds
  200 concurrent transfers -> all threads blocked
  Simple balance check arrives -> no thread available -> timeout
  CPU utilization: 5% (threads idle, just waiting on I/O)
  Service appears DOWN even though machine is idle!
```

### What is the accept-then-process pattern?

The endpoint does only the fast part: validate, persist a PENDING record, enqueue, and return immediately with HTTP 202 Accepted and a tracking handle.

```java
package com.bank.transfer;

import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PostMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestBody;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestHeader;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;
import java.net.URI;

@RestController
public class AsyncTransferController {

    private final TransferJobService jobService;

    public AsyncTransferController(TransferJobService jobService) {
        this.jobService = jobService;
    }

    @PostMapping("/api/transfers/international")
    public ResponseEntity<TransferJob> transfer(
            @RequestHeader("Idempotency-Key") String idempotencyKey,
            @RequestBody TransferRequest req) {

        // Fast path: validate + persist + enqueue (~10ms)
        TransferJob job = jobService.accept(idempotencyKey, req);

        // Return 202 Accepted with tracking URL
        return ResponseEntity
            .status(HttpStatus.ACCEPTED)
            .location(URI.create("/api/transfers/" + job.getId() +
                "/status"))
            .body(job);
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.transfer;

import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class TransferJobService {

    private final TransferJobRepository jobRepo;
    private final KafkaTemplate<String, TransferJob> kafka;

    public TransferJobService(TransferJobRepository jobRepo,
                               KafkaTemplate<String, TransferJob> kafka) {
        this.jobRepo = jobRepo;
        this.kafka = kafka;
    }

    @Transactional
    public TransferJob accept(String idempotencyKey,
                               TransferRequest req) {
        // Idempotency: return existing job if key already used
        TransferJob existing = jobRepo.findByIdempotencyKey(idempotencyKey);
        if (existing != null) {
            return existing;
        }

        // Create PENDING job
        TransferJob job = new TransferJob(
            idempotencyKey, req, TransferStatus.PENDING);
        jobRepo.save(job);

        // Enqueue for background processing
        kafka.send("transfer-jobs", job.getId(), job);

        return job;
    }
}
```

```text
HTTP Response:
  HTTP/1.1 202 Accepted
  Location: /api/transfers/TXF-12345/status
  Content-Type: application/json

  {
    "id": "TXF-12345",
    "status": "PENDING",
    "message": "Transfer accepted for processing"
  }

  Thread released in ~10ms (not 30-90 seconds!)
```

### How does the client track progress?

The client either polls the status endpoint or receives a webhook/push notification when the work completes.

```java
package com.bank.transfer;

import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PathVariable;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

@RestController
public class TransferStatusController {

    private final TransferJobRepository jobRepo;

    public TransferStatusController(TransferJobRepository jobRepo) {
        this.jobRepo = jobRepo;
    }

    @GetMapping("/api/transfers/{jobId}/status")
    public TransferJob getStatus(@PathVariable String jobId) {
        return jobRepo.findById(jobId)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new NotFoundException("Job not found"));
    }
}
```

```text
Client polling:
  t=0s:   POST /transfers/international -> 202 PENDING
  t=5s:   GET /transfers/TXF-12345/status -> PROCESSING
  t=30s:  GET /transfers/TXF-12345/status -> COMPLETED
  Or:
  Webhook: POST https://client.bank.com/callbacks/TXF-12345
           {"status": "COMPLETED", "settlementRef": "SWIFT-789"}
```

### What does async actually improve?

Async improves four things: throughput, decoupling, failure isolation, and user experience.

| Aspect | Synchronous REST | Accept-then-Process |
|---|---|---|
| Thread held | Entire duration | ~10ms |
| Throughput | Limited by thread pool | Limited by queue capacity |
| Failure handling | Client must retry whole flow | Failed job retried from queue |
| Scaling | API and workers coupled | API and workers scale independently |
| User experience | Spinner for 30-90 seconds | Immediate acknowledgment |
| Duplicate risk | Client timeout -> resubmit | Idempotency key -> safe retry |

### How does the queue act as a shock absorber?

The queue absorbs bursts while consumers drain at a steady rate. This smooths spikes instead of forcing every request to wait.

```text
Queue as shock absorber:
  Morning spike: 500 international transfers submitted in 1 minute
  Producer rate: 500/min (all accepted, all get 202)
  Consumer rate: 100/min (5 workers processing steadily)
  Queue depth: 400 pending (drains in ~4 minutes)

  Without queue: 500 threads blocked -> pool exhausted -> everything fails
  With queue: all 500 accepted in seconds, processed over 5 minutes
```

### Why is idempotency critical for the accept endpoint?

The client may retry the accept call if the 202 response is lost. The idempotency key ensures a retry does not create a duplicate job.

```java
package com.bank.transfer;

// WRONG: no idempotency key
// Client timeout -> retry -> two jobs created -> double transfer!
public TransferJob acceptWithoutIdempotency(TransferRequest req) {
    TransferJob job = new TransferJob(req, TransferStatus.PENDING);
    jobRepo.save(job);
    kafka.send("transfer-jobs", job);
    return job;
}

// CORRECT: idempotency key
// Client timeout -> retry with same key -> returns existing job
public TransferJob acceptWithIdempotency(String idempotencyKey,
                                          TransferRequest req) {
    TransferJob existing = jobRepo.findByIdempotencyKey(idempotencyKey);
    if (existing != null) return existing; // Safe retry
    TransferJob job = new TransferJob(idempotencyKey, req);
    jobRepo.save(job);
    kafka.send("transfer-jobs", job);
    return job;
}
```

### How is this different from reactive/non-blocking REST?

Reactive frameworks free the thread during I/O waits and help with throughput, but they do not solve the semantics of genuinely long-running, multi-minute workflows where accept-then-process with a durable job record is still the right model.

```text
Reactive REST:
  Thread freed during I/O wait -> handles more concurrent requests
  But: if SWIFT takes 30 seconds, the CLIENT still waits 30 seconds
  Good for: short I/O-bound calls (milliseconds)
  Not enough for: multi-minute workflows

Accept-then-process:
  Client gets 202 in 10ms and goes about its business
  Work happens in background at its own pace
  Right model for: anything > 1-2 seconds
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer uses the accept-then-process pattern not just for performance but for operational safety. A durable PENDING record means failed jobs can be retried from the queue without asking the customer to resubmit. The idempotency key prevents financial double-processing. The queue enables independent scaling of the API and workers.

```java
package com.bank.transfer;

public class SeniorGuidelines {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Senior guidelines for async workflows:");
        System.out.println("  1. Any work > 2 seconds: accept-then-process");
        System.out.println("  2. Always return a tracking handle (job ID or status URL)");
        System.out.println("  3. Always require an idempotency key for financial operations");
        System.out.println("  4. Persist PENDING record BEFORE enqueuing (crash-safe)");
        System.out.println("  5. Workers are independently scalable from the API");
        System.out.println("  6. Dead-letter queue for jobs that fail after N retries");
        System.out.println("  7. Monitor: queue depth, processing latency, DLQ size");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
